## Authentication
Bearer Token — Authentication method detected from captured traffic.

### Providing credentials
- Prefer passing auth as request headers or cookies at call time (do not hardcode secrets into the skill).
- If executing via an external proxy/executor, pass per-call credentials (cookies and/or headers) so they are not persisted.

## Dependencies
- Runtime capable of HTTPS requests.
- If using `scripts/api.ts`: TypeScript toolchain (or transpile to JS).

## Endpoints

### Auth Flow (2)
- `GET /{token}/{id}/{token}/v2/playlist/av/primary/prot/cXNyPTE/playlist.json` — Authenticate and retrieve a playlist.
- `POST /ablincoln/vuid` — Authenticate and retrieve a VUID.

### Data Read (2)
- `GET /rest/v1/properties` — Get a list of properties.
- `GET /seo/{seoId}/script` — Retrieve SEO script content by ID.

### Data Write (4)
- `POST /~api/analytics` — Submit analytics data.
- `POST /add/{addId}` — Add data using an ID.
- `POST /api/event` — Submit an event.
- `POST /seo/{seoId}/optimization` — Submit SEO optimization data for an SEO ID.

## Quick Start

### OpenClaw (Unbrowse) workflow
- Authenticate in the browser first (no credentials are shipped with this skill).
- Capture fresh auth locally: run `unbrowse_capture` on an authenticated page for this domain.
- Execute via backend gate + client browser executor: `unbrowse_replay` with `executionMode="backend"`.

Example:
```text
unbrowse_replay { service: "overnightly", executionMode: "backend", endpoint: "GET /rest/v1/properties" }
```

Here are a few examples to get you started. These assume you have a pre-configured `client` object with methods corresponding to the API endpoints.

```typescript
// Retrieve a list of properties.  This is a common read operation.
async function getProperties() {
  try {
    const properties = await client.get_rest_v1_properties();
    console.log("Properties:", properties);
    return properties;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error("Error fetching properties:", error);
    return null;
  }
}
```

```typescript
// Submit analytics data. This is a common write operation.
async function submitAnalytics(data: any) {
  try {
    const response = await client.post_api_analytics(data);
    console.log("Analytics submission response:", response);
    return response;
  } catch (error) {
    console.error("Error submitting analytics:", error);
    return null;
  }
}
```

